# Tiny Tales — Component Patterns

How to structure React Native components in this codebase. Follow these patterns consistently so all components feel like they came from the same hand.

---

## Component File Structure

Every component file follows this order — no exceptions:

```typescript
// 1. React & RN imports
import { View, Text, Pressable } from 'react-native'

// 2. Third-party imports
import { format } from 'date-fns'

// 3. Internal imports (lib, hooks, other components)
import { YELLOW } from '../lib/theme'
import { TagChip } from './TagChip'

// 4. Types
interface TaleCardProps {
  tale: {
    id: string
    title: string | null
    narrative: string
    created_at: string
    author: { name: string; avatar_url: string | null }
    tale_children: Array<{ child: { id: string; name: string; photo_url: string | null } }>
    tale_tags: Array<{ tag: string }>
  }
  onPress: (id: string) => void
}

// 5. Component (named export, not default)
export function TaleCard({ tale, onPress }: TaleCardProps) {
  // hooks first
  // derived values / handlers next
  // return JSX last
}
```

Always use **named exports** for components. Never use `export default` for components (Expo Router screens are the only exception — they require default exports).

---

## Screen File Structure

Expo Router screens (files inside `app/`) use default exports and follow this pattern:

```typescript
import { View, FlatList } from 'react-native'
import { useLocalSearchParams } from 'expo-router'

// Internal
import { useTaleDetail } from '../../hooks/useTaleDetail'
import { TaleDetailSkeleton } from '../../components/TaleDetailSkeleton'
import { ErrorMessage } from '../../components/ErrorMessage'

export default function TaleDetailScreen() {
  const { id } = useLocalSearchParams<{ id: string }>()
  const { tale, loading, error, refetch } = useTaleDetail(id)

  if (loading) return <TaleDetailSkeleton />
  if (error) return <ErrorMessage onRetry={refetch} />
  if (!tale) return <ErrorMessage message="Tale not found" />

  return (
    <View className="flex-1 bg-white">
      {/* content */}
    </View>
  )
}
```

---

## The Three-State Rule

Every component that loads data must render correctly in all three states. Handle them at the top of the component before the main return — no ternaries buried inside JSX for loading/error states.

```typescript
// ✅ Correct — guard clauses at the top
if (loading) return <TaleCardSkeleton />
if (error) return <ErrorMessage onRetry={refetch} />
if (!tales.length) return <EmptyFeed />

return ( /* main content */ )

// ❌ Wrong — buried ternaries
return (
  <View>
    {loading ? <Spinner /> : error ? <Text>Error</Text> : tales.map(...)}
  </View>
)
```

---

## Skeleton Components

Every list item or card component that loads async data must have a corresponding skeleton:

- `TaleCard.tsx` → `TaleCardSkeleton.tsx`
- `CommentItem.tsx` → `CommentItemSkeleton.tsx`

Skeletons use `className="bg-gray-200 rounded animate-pulse"` blocks to represent the content shape. Show 3 skeletons for any list.

---

## Pressable vs TouchableOpacity

Always use `Pressable` — not `TouchableOpacity`, not `TouchableHighlight`.

```typescript
<Pressable
  onPress={handlePress}
  className="active:opacity-70"
>
  <Text className="text-base font-medium text-gray-900">{tale.title}</Text>
</Pressable>
```

---

## Buttons

Use a consistent `Button` component from `components/Button.tsx`. Never build ad-hoc buttons in screens.

```typescript
// components/Button.tsx
interface ButtonProps {
  label: string
  onPress: () => void
  variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary' | 'destructive'
  loading?: boolean
  disabled?: boolean
  fullWidth?: boolean
}
```

- `primary` — brand yellow background, dark text (main CTAs)
- `secondary` — white background, dark border, dark text
- `destructive` — red background, white text (delete, revoke)

---

## Forms & Inputs

- Use `TextInput` from React Native — never a third-party input library
- Wrap all form screens in `KeyboardAvoidingView` with `behavior="padding"` on iOS
- Show validation errors inline below the field, in red, only after the user has interacted with the field (`onBlur` triggers validation, not `onChange`)

```typescript
<View className="mb-4">
  <Text className="text-sm font-medium text-gray-700 mb-1">Name</Text>
  <TextInput
    value={name}
    onChangeText={setName}
    onBlur={() => setTouched(true)}
    className="border border-gray-300 rounded-xl px-4 py-3 text-base text-gray-900"
    placeholder="Child's name"
  />
  {touched && !name && (
    <Text className="text-sm text-red-500 mt-1">Name is required</Text>
  )}
</View>
```

---

## Modals & Bottom Sheets

- Use `Modal` from React Native for full-screen confirmation modals (e.g. delete account)
- Use `@gorhom/bottom-sheet` for bottom sheets (tag picker, filter sheet)
- Always include a dismiss gesture (swipe down or tap backdrop)
- Confirmation modals must have an explicit Cancel button — never rely solely on swipe-to-dismiss

---

## Role-Based Rendering

Check `currentUser.role` from the Zustand store to conditionally show parent-only controls. Never show edit/delete to guests.

```typescript
import { useStore } from '../lib/store'

const { currentUser } = useStore()
const isParent = currentUser.role === 'parent'

// In JSX:
{isParent && (
  <Pressable onPress={handleEdit}>
    <EditIcon />
  </Pressable>
)}
```

---

## Avatar Component

Use a shared `Avatar` component for all user and child avatars. It handles missing photos with an initial-based fallback.

```typescript
// components/Avatar.tsx
interface AvatarProps {
  uri?: string | null
  name: string
  size?: 'sm' | 'md' | 'lg'  // 32, 44, 64px
}
```

---

## Date Formatting

Always use `date-fns` — never `new Date().toLocaleDateString()`.

```typescript
import { formatDistanceToNow, format } from 'date-fns'

// Relative (feed): "2 hours ago"
formatDistanceToNow(new Date(tale.created_at), { addSuffix: true })

// Absolute (detail): "Monday 1 September 2025"
format(new Date(tale.created_at), 'EEEE d MMMM yyyy')
```

---

## Empty States

Every empty state gets a friendly message and, where appropriate, a CTA. Never render a blank screen.

```typescript
// components/EmptyFeed.tsx
export function EmptyFeed() {
  return (
    <View className="flex-1 items-center justify-center px-8 py-16">
      <Text className="text-4xl mb-4">📖</Text>
      <Text className="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900 text-center mb-2">
        No tales yet
      </Text>
      <Text className="text-base text-gray-500 text-center mb-6">
        Record your first moment — it only takes a minute.
      </Text>
      <Button label="Tell a tale" onPress={() => router.push('/(app)/tale/new')} />
    </View>
  )
}
```
